**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across PMO, MOFA, MOD, Diet transcripts, IAEA/MOE/METI material, Reuters, Kyodo, Nippon/republished Reuters, and a few later retrospective pages used only to find the right primary 2022 record.
- I retained 15 source-claim units from 15 URLs because they were the cleanest mix of primary, independent-monitoring, and nuclear-safety evidence for 2022.
- I rejected duplicate copies of the same speech or statement when the PMO version was cleaner than a mirror.
- I rejected later retrospective speeches from 2023-2024 as direct evidence because they were outside the target year, even when they help confirm the same policy line.
- I rejected broad background pages on Japan's security policy when they did not add ruler-attributed 2022 choice beyond the Constitution or the 2022 statements.
- I rejected commentary-heavy pieces when an official Diet transcript or PMO transcript already captured the same point more directly.
- I did not use the client matrix, and I did not re-fetch local structured country-year datasets already supplied in the thread.

**Remaining gaps**
- Internal cabinet deliberations on whether Kishida personally weighed nuclear-sharing, extended-deterrence, or counterstrike tradeoffs are still thin; the public record mostly shows his public line.
- The ALPS treated-water and nuclear-energy restart material shows safety procedure and transparency, but not enough to distinguish how much was driven personally by Kishida versus his ministers and the bureaucracy.
- I still do not have direct evidence of any 2022 Kishida backchannel that materially de-escalated a nuclear crisis beyond public diplomacy.
- If you want, I can do one more focused pass for Cabinet or Diet records on `extended deterrence`, `counterstrike`, or `nuclear sharing` and tighten the chapter evidence set further.

